# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two
# FILES, never a shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is
# defined in run_gates.sh. Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_extractor_gorm_ent.py" "GORM-ENT GATE: PASS" "Go gorm + ent ORM extractor" "Go gorm (gorm.Model embedding, TableName() return, gorm struct field tags) and ent (ent.Schema + Fields/Annotations, entsql.Annotation{Table:...}) both mint table nodes and alters/queries edges, lighting up the cross-substrate crown jewel for Go repos." "Adds Go gorm and ent schema extraction to _cg_schema so these ORM frameworks mint table nodes (and alters/queries edges) instead of 0. THE GAP: _ORM_PATTERNS covered Django/SQLAlchemy/JPA/Rails/Laravel plus raw SQL but not Go. gorm models use struct embedding (gorm.Model) or field tags (gorm:\"...\") to mark DB entities; ent schemas embed ent.Schema and define Fields() methods. Both patterns use snake_case plural of the struct name as the default table name; explicit overrides (TableName() return value, entsql.Annotation{Table:...}) take precedence. WHAT THIS GATE PROVES (hermetic, no DB, no Postgres, no network): (1) gorm explicit TableName() return value mints the exact table name; (2) gorm.Model embedding mints snake_case-plural table name; (3) gorm struct field tag mints snake_case-plural table name; (4) ent entsql.Annotation{Table:...} mints exact explicit name; (5) ent.Schema + Fields() mints snake_case-plural table name; (6) PRECISION - plain Go struct with no ORM marker does NOT mint a table node; (7) PRECISION - Go doc-comment code examples do NOT fire (line-comment stripping prevents false positives from framework documentation like ent.go itself); (8) CROSS-SUBSTRATE CROWN JEWEL: a gorm migration file (alters edge, migrations/ dir) and a gorm model file (queries edge) are coupled via the shared table node with CONFIRMED absence of any calls/imports edge between them; (9) never-crash: empty Go file, binary-ish content, non-Go files do not raise; (10) content-free: no file body is emitted. PRECISION GUARDS: (a) gorm.Model embedding requires the EXACT string 'gorm.Model' anchored at word boundary inside the struct body (not 'mygorm.Model'); (b) gorm tag pattern requires backtick-quoted 'gorm:\"...\"' field tag inside a struct body bounded to 2048 chars; (c) ent.Schema requires EXACT 'ent.Schema' anchor plus a file-level guard that the file contains at least one Fields() or Annotations() method definition (prevents mixin stubs and the ent framework's own type declarations from minting spurious tables); (d) Go single-line comments are stripped before all Go-specific patterns fire; (e) known framework-internal struct names (Model, Schema, single letters) are in a skip list. MEASURED on real repos: gitea (uses gorm) 27 real table nodes from .go migration/model files (user/oauth2_grant/action/notification/issue/comment/mirror/team/webauthn_credential etc); go-ent library 75 table nodes from integration-test schemas (users/groups/pets/cards/payments/sessions etc); gorm library itself 148 nodes mostly from test structs (expected for a framework repo with no user app models)." 20
